salah abdeslam sat expressionless as the  judge delivered the verdicts the only  surviving suspected attacker was found  guilty of terrorism and murder and  sentenced to life in prison without  parole during the trial the belgian-born  frenchman said that he had decided not  to blow himself up  but the judge said the court considered  that the explosive vest had  malfunctioned abduslam's childhood  friend mohamed abrini who drove him from  paris to brussels on the night of the  attacks was also given a life sentence  he was also found guilty of terrorism as  were 18 other men one other was found  guilty of fraud  the sentences are not excessive they  reflect the events and those behind them  i think it's a satisfying moment for  everyone and for the justice system  it was nearly seven years ago that 10  attackers rampaged across paris killing  people in cafes the bataclan concert  hall and outside france's national  sports stadium by the end of the night  130 people were dead hundreds injured  isil claimed responsibility across  france there was shock and grief  the trial into the paris attacks was the  biggest in french history it lasted  nearly 10 months hundreds of witnesses  testified and while some victims chose  to stay away others came to the court  nearly every day  muhammad amgar was injured when an  attacker blew himself up outside the  stadium he says he found it helpful to  attend the trial most days now it's over  he hopes that he can move forward i will  close the book and i will go on my life  i will try to to keep going  this this story this this this thing  happened in my life it was part of my  life so i have to live with i have no  choice so i have to live with i accepted  before it was very hard for me to accept  it but i have to accept it now it's  about me  abdeslam will serve his sentence in  france or belgium where he's already in  prison for the victims survivors and  their relatives the verdicts won't  change the past bring back loved ones or  heal their suffering but for some there  will at least be a sense that finally  justice has been served natasha butler  al jazeera paris
 

